# whereverwhenever

*Your iPhone, wherever, whenever.*

Spoof an iPhone's GPS location from a Windows/Linux/Mac box, and make the fake
location **move like a real person**: on real roads, braking for corners, waiting at
lights, lingering at each stop, with GPS drift. No jailbreak, no admin, no paid app.
A map UI, a CLI, and a deterministic motion engine. Built on
[pymobiledevice3](https://github.com/doronz88/pymobiledevice3).

Teleporting a phone to a pin is one API call that every "location changer" app makes.
What none of them do is the part that makes a spoof survive a glance from a human or a
heuristic: a trajectory that could have been recorded by a phone in someone's pocket.
That is what `motion.py` is.

> **Status (Nov 2026):** works on iOS 17.4 through 26.x for Maps and most apps.
> On **iOS 27.2** the phone accepts the command and ignores it. **Find My** stopped
> honouring simulated location around iOS 26.6 regardless. See [The hack](#the-hack)
> and [Workarounds](#workarounds) before you spend time on this.

## How it works

Apple ships a developer feature that lets Xcode inject a fake CoreLocation fix into a
paired phone, so developers can test location-aware apps. It's reached over the
iOS 17+ developer tunnel through the DVT instruments channel
(`com.apple.instruments.server.services.LocationSimulation`). Every commercial
"location changer" app for iPhone is a wrapper around this same channel.

whereverwhenever does three things:

1. Opens a no-root, in-process tunnel to the phone over USB (pymobiledevice3's
   userspace tunnel).
2. Opens the LocationSimulation channel and sends `simulateLocationWithLatitude:longitude:`.
3. **Keeps that session open.** The phone only honours the fake location while the
   session lives. Drop the cable, sleep the PC, kill the script, and real GPS is back
   within seconds. whereverwhenever reconnects and re-applies the last pin on any drop.

## Use

Phone plugged in and unlocked, then:

```
pip install git+https://github.com/angadsingharora/whereverwhenever
whereverwhenever
```

That starts the **bridge**, the one local process that talks to the phone, and opens the
UI. The same UI is hosted at **https://whereverwhenever.vercel.app** and talks to the bridge
on your machine; if the bridge isn't running the site tells you how to start it.

First time only: iPhone Settings > Privacy & Security > Developer Mode > on, reboot
(if the toggle is missing: `pymobiledevice3 amfi reveal-developer-mode`). Windows needs
the **Apple Devices** app from the Store for the USB driver. Then
`pymobiledevice3 mounter auto-mount` once.

**Teleport:** click the map, **Teleport**. Search a place or paste `lat, lng`.

**Itinerary:** add stops (click the map with **+ Add stop** on, search, or **Start from
pin**), set minutes to linger at each, Walk / Bike / Drive, optional cruise mph, optional
loop. **Plan route** fetches real roads and draws the speed profile the phone will follow,
with every stop, red light and corner-limited bend marked; hover it to see where on the map
that is. **Go** runs it. Live speed, acceleration, heading, next stop, and a cursor on the
profile. **Stop here** freezes it. The bridge does the moving, so the tab can be closed;
a running itinerary is saved to `~/.whereverwhenever/state.json` and resumes mid-route
after a restart.

**Plan in words:** with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` set and `pip install anthropic`, a text box
appears: "gym for an hour, coffee at Blue Bottle, then home, by bike". Claude (Opus 5.5)
turns it into stops, mode and dwell times; the bridge geocodes each place itself, so the
model never invents coordinates. You review the stops before anything moves.

CLI (same engine, no UI):
```
whereverwhenever-cli pin 37.8716 -122.2727
whereverwhenever-cli go 37.8716 -122.2727 37.8044 -122.2712 --mode drive   # A to B on roads, hold at B
whereverwhenever-cli plan day.json --mode walk --loop   # [{lat,lng,name,dwell_s}, ...] round and round
whereverwhenever-cli walk route.gpx --speed 1.4         # raw GPX track, no motion model
whereverwhenever-cli clear
```
`--seed N` replays the exact same lights, pauses and drift. `--straight` skips routing (offline).
From a checkout, `python geoweb.py` and `python geofake.py` do the same.

## Motion model

`whereverwhenever/motion.py` turns a list of stops into a position-as-a-function-of-time that is
deterministic from a seed, so "where should the phone be at 14:07:32" has one answer on
any machine. Pipeline, per itinerary:

1. **Routing.** Each leg goes through OSRM (`routing.openstreetmap.de`) with the car, bike
   or foot profile, so a walk cuts through the park and a drive respects one-way streets.
   Maneuver points (turns, roundabouts, junctions) are kept as candidate stop points.
   Offline or no road nearby: straight line for that leg, flagged in the UI.
2. **Speed ceiling.** Cruise speed wanders ±12–15 % every 150–400 m (nobody holds exactly
   30 mph). At every vertex the heading change gives a curvature; speed through it is capped
   by lateral acceleration (`v ≤ √(a_lat / κ)`), with a hard cap through sharp turns.
3. **Stops.** Itinerary stops force v = 0 and a dwell. Each maneuver point stops with a
   per-mode probability (35 % driving) for 4–40 s: lights and stop signs.
4. **Feasibility.** A forward pass enforces acceleration limits, a backward pass braking
   limits, so the car can't be doing 30 at a corner it has to take at 9. This yields
   keyframes `(t, s, v)` with constant acceleration between them.
5. **Sampling.** `pos_at(t)` bisects the keyframes, integrates the kinematics to an arc
   length, maps that onto the polyline, and returns lat, lng, heading, speed, leg, progress.
6. **GPS drift.** Real fixes wander a few metres on a 10–90 s timescale, not white noise.
   A seeded sum of sinusoids per axis (normalised to 3–5 m RMS by mode) gives that with no
   state, so it's resumable and identical on replay.

`tests/test_motion.py` checks all of it offline: exact arrival, monotone time and
distance, accel/decel limits between every 1 s sample, corner slow-down, dwell
duration, loop wrap, seed determinism, JSON round-trip, noise RMS and smoothness.
Run `python -m pytest -q`.

Honest limits: no elevation, no traffic, no lane-level detail, the road graph is whatever
OSM has, and iOS still only gets a lat/lng (no speed or course is injected through this
channel, apps derive those from successive fixes).

Both take `--rsd ADDR PORT` to reuse a tunnel from `pymobiledevice3 lockdown start-tunnel`,
and `--udid` if more than one phone is attached. `geoweb.py --no-device` runs the UI
without a phone.

## Verify

1. Status box in the sidebar goes green with your iPhone model and iOS version.
2. Apple Maps on the phone jumps to the pin. If it doesn't, the phone is ignoring the spoof.
3. For Find My, check from the **outside**: iCloud.com > Find My, or a friend's phone.
   Watch for a full minute. Known behaviour on 26.6+: flashes the fake spot for half a
   second, then snaps back to real.

## The hack

What this exploits, and where it stops:

- **It's an official feature, not a bug.** Developer Mode and location simulation are
  Apple-sanctioned. Nothing here voids warranty or touches the OS. Fully reversible.
- **It's tethered.** The computer must be in the loop the whole time, over USB or the
  same Wi-Fi. There is no way to make the phone remember a fake location on its own
  without a jailbreak, and there is no jailbreak for current hardware.
- **It can't run on the phone.** iOS won't let an app send the phone its own developer
  commands, and there's no public API to override system location.
- **iOS 27.2 ignores it.** Tested on iPhone18,3 / 27.2 with pymobiledevice3 11.43: tunnel
  up, channel open, `set` accepted with no error, phone doesn't move. The developer
  image available is the iOS 27.0 beta build (27A5228h), the newest the tooling ships.
  Whether Apple closed the channel or the image is just too old for 27.2 is unknown.
  The legacy pre-17 service (`com.apple.dt.simulatelocation`) is gone from the phone.
- **Find My is a separate wall.** Even where Maps moves (iOS 26.x), Find My reverts to
  the real location within a second. Multiple independent reports since 26.6. Apple
  appears to have moved Find My's reporting off the path that simulation overrides.
- **Offline leak.** If the phone drops off the network, the Find My network reports its
  real position via nearby Apple devices regardless of any spoof.
- **Anti-spoof apps detect it.** Pokémon Go, some banks and dating apps check for
  simulated location and ban.

## Workarounds

For "my friend sees me somewhere else in Find My", in order of how well they actually work:

1. **Second iPhone.** Sign a spare phone into your Apple ID, leave it where you want to
   appear, set it as the sharing device: Settings > your name > Find My > My Location.
   No computer, no cable, no iOS version dependency. This is the only one that holds up.
2. **Pause sharing.** Find My > People > the person > Stop Sharing, or toggle Share My
   Location off. Shows "No location found" rather than a fake one.
3. **This tool, on iOS 26.x or earlier,** for apps other than Find My. Keep the PC awake
   and the cable in.
4. **Wait for tooling.** Check `pip install -U pymobiledevice3` occasionally. If
   `pymobiledevice3 mounter list` ever shows a developer image newer than 27A5228h, retry.

Things that don't work: GPS RF spoofing with an SDR (illegal to transmit nearly
everywhere, and the phone cross-checks Wi-Fi and cell positioning anyway), browser
geolocation extensions (fool websites only), any paid "location changer" app (same
channel, same limits).

## Files

- `whereverwhenever/motion.py`: routing + motion model + GPS drift, deterministic and serialisable
- `whereverwhenever/server.py`: the bridge (HTTP API, device loop, persistence, AI planner)
- `whereverwhenever/page.html`: the UI; `site/index.html` is the copy Vercel serves
- `whereverwhenever/cli.py`: CLI and the shared `Session` class
- `tests/test_motion.py`: offline checks on the motion model
